s not sargable.** A plain index on `users.email` can't be used, so the database will probably scan all of `users` and apply `lower()` to every row. This is the main problem.\n   - **Fix, option A:** create an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));` (Postgres or Oracle). In MySQL 8.0.13+, use a functional index: `((lower(email)))`.\n   - **Fix, option B:** store emails normalized to lowercase, or use a case-insensitive type or collation (`citext` in Postgres, or a `_ci` collation in MySQL). Then use `WHERE u.email = ...` with a normal unique index.\n   - Whichever you choose, the query's expression has to match the index's expression exactly.\n\n2. **`SELECT *` pulls every column from both tables.** This has several costs:\n   - It sends more data over the wire.\n   - It rules out index-only scans.\n   - It returns duplicate column names (`id` and others), which can break clients that map columns by name.\n   - It breaks if the schema changes.\n\n   List only the columns you need.\n\n3. **`orders.user_id` needs an index.** If it isn't indexed, the join can fall back to a scan of `orders` (a hash join or a nested loop with a seq scan per user). Foreign keys aren't indexed automatically in Postgres or MySQL/InnoDB (InnoDB does create one, but Postgres doesn't). Make sure `CREATE INDEX ON orders (user_id);` exists.\n\n4. **There's no `LIMIT` or ordering.** If one user can have many orders, this returns all of them. For a UI or API, add `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT n`. A composite index on `(user_id, created_at)` then serves both the join and the sort.\n\n**Suggested rewrite** (Postgres; column names are placeholders):\n\n```sql\n-- indexes\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_lower_uq ON users (lower(email));\nCREATE INDEX orders_user_created_idx ON orders (user_id, created_at DESC);\n\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org'\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n```\n\nI only had the SQL file, with no schema, indexes, or `EXPLAIN` output. These points come from the query's shape, so check them with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` against real data. If `users.email` already has a functional index and `orders.user_id` is indexed, the query is fine apart from `SELECT *` and the missing `LIMIT`.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}